<!-- ===================== Problem 1 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="ex1-p1">
<title>4 points</title>
<introduction>
<p>
The centerline of an arch is modeled by the curve
<md>
y = 50 - 30\cosh\left(\frac{x}{30}\right),
</md>
where <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are measured in meters. The ground is the line
<m>y = 0</m>, and the arch is the part of the curve with <m>y \ge 0</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex1-arch">
<caption>The arch <m>y = 50 - 30\cosh\left(\frac{x}{30}\right)</m> above the ground <m>y = 0</m>.</caption>
<image width="60%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-ex1-arch">
<diagram dimensions="(400,190)" margins="10">
<definition>f(x) = 50 - 30*cosh(x/30)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-55,-8,55,28)">
<axes at="axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<graph at="arch" function="f" domain="(-32.96,32.96)" stroke="black" thickness="2.5"/>
<label at="ground-label" anchor="(-33,-1)" alignment="sw"><m>\text{ground}</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="An arch drawn above a horizontal ground line, the x axis. The curve rises from the ground on the left to a single highest point on the y axis and comes back down to the ground on the right, symmetric about the y axis.">
  <annotation ref="arch" text="The arch y equals 50 minus 30 times hyperbolic cosine of x over 30, drawn where it lies above the ground."/>
  <annotation ref="axes" text="The x axis is the ground line y equals 0; the y axis is the axis of symmetry of the arch."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="ex1-p1-a">
<statement>
<p>
Find the height of the arch, that is, its maximum height above the ground.
Justify why this is the maximum. (1 point)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<m>\cosh u \ge 1</m>, with equality only at <m>u = 0</m>, so
<md>
y = 50 - 30\cosh\left(\frac{x}{30}\right) \le 50 - 30 = 20,
</md>
with equality only at <m>x = 0</m>. Height: <alert>20 m</alert>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="ex1-p1-b">
<statement>
<p>
Find the width of the arch at ground level. (2 points)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<m>y = 0 \iff \cosh\frac{x}{30} = \frac{u + u^{-1}}{2} = \frac53</m>, where
<m>u = e^{x/30} \gt 0</m>. So
<md>
3u^2 - 10u + 3 = (3u - 1)(u - 3) = 0,
</md>
<m>u = 3</m> or <m>\frac13</m>, and <m>x = 30\ln u = \pm 30\ln 3</m>. Width:
<md>
30\ln 3 - (-30\ln 3) = 60\ln 3\ \text{m} \quad (\approx 66\ \text{m}).
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="ex1-p1-c">
<statement>
<p>
Find the slope of the arch where it meets the ground on the right. (1 point)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<m>y' = -30\sinh\frac{x}{30}\cdot\frac{1}{30} = -\sinh\frac{x}{30}</m>; at the
right base <m>x = 30\ln 3</m>,
<md>
y' = -\sinh(\ln 3) = -\frac{e^{\ln 3} - e^{-\ln 3}}{2} = -\frac{3 - \frac13}{2} = -\frac43.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Problem 2 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="ex1-p2">
<title>4 points</title>
<introduction>
<p>
For each series below, determine whether it converges or diverges. If it
converges, find its sum. Remember to fully justify your answers.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="ex1-p2-a">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{\sinh(n)}{e^{n}}</m> (2 points)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
a_n = \frac{\sinh n}{e^{n}} = \frac{e^{n} - e^{-n}}{2e^{n}} = \frac{1 - e^{-2n}}{2} \to \frac12 \ne 0,
</md>
so the series <alert>diverges</alert> by the <m>n</m>th Term Test.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="ex1-p2-b">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{\sinh(n)}{e^{3n}}</m> (2 points)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
\frac{\sinh n}{e^{3n}} = \frac{e^{n} - e^{-n}}{2e^{3n}} = \frac12\left(e^{-2n} - e^{-4n}\right),
</md>
and for <m>k = 2, 4</m>,
<md>
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} e^{-kn} = \frac{e^{-k}}{1 - e^{-k}} = \frac{1}{e^{k} - 1}
</md>
(geometric, <m>a = r = e^{-k}</m>, <m>|r| \lt 1</m>). By the difference and
constant multiple rules, the series <alert>converges</alert> to
<md>
<mrow>\frac12\left(\frac{1}{e^2 - 1} - \frac{1}{e^4 - 1}\right)
\amp = \frac12\cdot\frac{(e^2 + 1) - 1}{e^4 - 1}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{e^2}{2\left(e^4 - 1\right)}
\quad \left(= \frac{1}{4\sinh 2} \approx 0.069\right).</mrow>
</md>
(The <m>n</m>th Term Test is inconclusive here: the terms tend to <m>0</m>.)
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Problem 3 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="ex1-p3">
<title>3 points</title>
<introduction>
<p>
A patient takes a 60 mg dose of a medication at the same time every day. By
the time the next dose is taken, the body has eliminated three quarters of
the medication, so exactly <m>\frac14</m> of the amount that was in the body
just after a dose is still there when the next dose is taken. Let
<m>Q_n</m> be the amount of medication (in mg) in the body just after the
<m>n</m>th dose, so that <m>Q_1 = 60</m>.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="ex1-p3-a">
<statement>
<p>
Find a formula for <m>Q_n</m> that does not contain a sum (no
<q><m>\cdots</m></q> and no <m>\sum</m>). (2 points)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<m>Q_n = 60 + \frac14 Q_{n-1}</m> (the new dose plus the quarter left), so
<md>
Q_n = 60 + 60\left(\tfrac14\right) + \cdots + 60\left(\tfrac14\right)^{n-1},
</md>
the <m>n</m>th partial sum of the geometric series with <m>a = 60</m>,
<m>r = \frac14</m>:
<md>
<mrow>Q_n \amp = 60\cdot\frac{1 - \left(\frac14\right)^{n}}{1 - \frac14}
= 80\left(1 - \left(\tfrac14\right)^{n}\right)\ \text{mg}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \left(\text{check: } Q_1 = 80\cdot\tfrac34 = 60,\ Q_2 = 80\cdot\tfrac{15}{16} = 75\right).</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="ex1-p3-b">
<statement>
<p>
What happens to <m>Q_n</m> in the long run? (1 point)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<m>\left(\frac14\right)^{n} \to 0</m>, so <m>Q_n \to 80</m> mg: the amount
just after a dose levels off at (approaches, but never exceeds) 80 mg, the
sum
<md>
\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} 60\left(\tfrac14\right)^{k-1} = \frac{60}{1 - \frac14}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Problem 4 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="ex1-p4">
<title>5 points</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Let <m>f(x) = \ln x</m>.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="ex1-p4-a">
<statement>
<p>
Find the Taylor polynomial <m>p_3(x)</m> of order 3 generated by <m>f</m> at
<m>x = 1</m>. (2 points)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<m>f' = x^{-1}</m>, <m>f'' = -x^{-2}</m>, <m>f''' = 2x^{-3}</m>, so
<m>f(1) = 0</m>, <m>f'(1) = 1</m>, <m>f''(1) = -1</m>, <m>f'''(1) = 2</m>, and
<md>
<mrow>p_3(x) \amp = (x-1) + \frac{-1}{2!}(x-1)^2 + \frac{2}{3!}(x-1)^3</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = (x-1) - \frac{(x-1)^2}{2} + \frac{(x-1)^3}{3}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="ex1-p4-b">
<statement>
<p>
For each <m>n</m>, let <m>p_n(x)</m> be the Taylor polynomial of order
<m>n</m> generated by <m>f</m> at <m>x = 1</m>. Use the Remainder
Estimation Theorem to find the smallest <m>n</m> for which the theorem
guarantees that the absolute value of the error in approximating
<m>\ln x</m> by <m>p_n(x)</m> is no greater than <m>0.01</m> for every
<m>x</m> in <m>\left[1, \frac32\right]</m>. (3 points)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<m>f^{(k)}(x) = (-1)^{k-1}\dfrac{(k-1)!}{x^{k}}</m> (<m>k \ge 1</m>). For
<m>x \in \left[1, \frac32\right]</m>, <m>c</m> is between <m>1</m> and
<m>x</m>, so <m>1 \le c \le \frac32</m> and
<md>
\left|f^{(n+1)}(c)\right| = \frac{n!}{c^{n+1}} \le n! = M
</md>
(largest at <m>c = 1</m>). With <m>|x - 1| \le \frac12</m>, the Remainder
Estimation Theorem gives
<md>
<mrow>\left|\ln x - p_n(x)\right| = \left|R_n(x)\right|
\amp \le \frac{M\,|x-1|^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \le \frac{n!}{(n+1)!}\left(\frac12\right)^{n+1} = \frac{1}{(n+1)\,2^{n+1}},</mrow>
</md>
which is <m>\le 0.01</m> if and only if <m>(n+1)\,2^{n+1} \ge 100</m>:
<m>n = 3</m> gives <m>4\cdot 16 = 64</m> (too small), <m>n = 4</m> gives
<m>5\cdot 32 = 160</m>. Smallest: <alert><m>n = 4</m></alert>. (Indeed
<m>p_3</m> fails:
<m>\left|\ln\frac32 - p_3\left(\frac32\right)\right| = \left|\ln\frac32 - \frac{5}{12}\right| \approx 0.0112</m>.)
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Problem 5 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="ex1-p5">
<title>4 points</title>

<task xml:id="ex1-p5-a">
<statement>
<p>
Use Taylor series to evaluate
<md>
\lim_{x\to 0} \frac{x - \sin x}{x\,(1 - \cos x)}.
</md>
(2 points)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>x - \sin x \amp = x - \left(x - \frac{x^3}{3!} + \frac{x^5}{5!} - \cdots\right) = \frac{x^3}{6} - \frac{x^5}{120} + \cdots,</mrow>
<mrow>x\,(1 - \cos x) \amp = x\left(\frac{x^2}{2!} - \frac{x^4}{4!} + \cdots\right) = \frac{x^3}{2} - \frac{x^5}{24} + \cdots,</mrow>
</md>
so, dividing by <m>x^3</m>, the limit is
<md>
\lim_{x\to 0} \frac{\frac16 - \frac{x^2}{120} + \cdots}{\frac12 - \frac{x^2}{24} + \cdots}
= \frac{1/6}{1/2} = \frac13.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="ex1-p5-b">
<statement>
<p>
<alert>True or False?</alert> If <m>\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n</m> and
<m>\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} b_n</m> both diverge, then
<m>\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} (a_n + b_n)</m> diverges. Justify your answer fully:
give a proof if the statement is true, or a counterexample if it is false.
(2 points)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<alert>False.</alert> Let <m>a_n = 1</m>, <m>b_n = -1</m>. Both series
diverge by the <m>n</m>th Term Test (<m>a_n \to 1 \ne 0</m>,
<m>b_n \to -1 \ne 0</m>; partial sums <m>n</m> and <m>-n</m>), but
<m>a_n + b_n = 0</m>, so <m>\sum (a_n + b_n) = 0</m> converges. (The Sum Rule
<m>\sum (a_n + b_n) = \sum a_n + \sum b_n</m> holds only when both series
converge.)
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Answers at a glance ===================== -->
<assemblage xml:id="ex1-answers">
<title>Answers at a glance</title>
<p>
<ol>
<li><p>(a) <m>20</m> m; (b) <m>60\ln 3</m> m; (c) <m>-\frac43</m>.</p></li>
<li><p>(a) diverges (the terms tend to <m>\frac12</m>); (b) converges to <m>\dfrac{e^2}{2\left(e^4 - 1\right)} = \dfrac{1}{4\sinh 2}</m>.</p></li>
<li><p>(a) <m>Q_n = 80\left(1 - \left(\frac14\right)^{n}\right)</m> mg; (b) <m>80</m> mg.</p></li>
<li><p>(a) <m>p_3(x) = (x-1) - \frac{(x-1)^2}{2} + \frac{(x-1)^3}{3}</m>; (b) <m>n = 4</m>.</p></li>
<li><p>(a) <m>\frac13</m>; (b) False (for example, <m>a_n = 1</m> and <m>b_n = -1</m>).</p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</assemblage>

</worksheet>
